The value, verified conceptually:

```js
// JS — the bug ships to production, found by a user at 2am
function getUserName(user) { return user.name.toUpperCase(); }
getUserName({});   // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined

// TS — the bug is caught before the code runs
type User = { name: string };
function getUserName(user: User): string { return user.name.toUpperCase(); }
getUserName({});   // COMPILE ERROR: Property 'name' is missing
// tsc emits plain JS with all types erased — zero runtime cost.
```

The four concrete wins (the interview answer):
- Catch errors early: typos, wrong shapes, undefined access — at compile time, where they cost nothing
- Editor intelligence: autocomplete on object shapes, safe renames across a codebase, inline docs via hover — the types ARE the documentation
- Refactor confidence: change a type, and the compiler lists every broken call site — this is what makes large-codebase maintenance feasible
- Onboarding: a function's signature tells you what it needs and returns without reading its body

The costs (name them — interviewers check for honesty): a build/compile step, fighting the type system for genuinely dynamic code, dependency .d.ts files, and stricter review. The design decision: types are ERASED at compile time — `const x: number = 1` becomes `const x = 1`. All checking happens before the code ships.
